The mid-sixth century for his birth date was proposed in [KQVXHPVH].</note></person><person xml:id="person3780"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dharmottara</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">chos mchog</persName><birth notBefore="0740-01-01">after 740</birth><death notAfter="0800-12-31">before 800</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Indic</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P9950</idno><idno type="APIS">3780</idno><note type="comment">Proposed approximate dates: 740-800, 751-782, 775-806 (see [AAIEJBMW]).</note></person><person xml:id="person3786"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rngog blo ldan shes rab</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">རྔོག་བློ་ལྡན་ཤེས་རབ</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rngog lo tsA ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">lo chen</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dge slong blo ldan shes rab</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rngog lo rtsa ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rngog dge slong blo ldan shes rab</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">blo ldan bzang po</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">lo tsha ba chen po</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">lo ts+tsha ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">lo tsha ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">lo tsa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rngog</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">blo ldan</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">ma hA</persName><birth notBefore="1059-01-01" notAfter="1059-12-31">1059</birth><death notBefore="1109-01-01" notAfter="1109-12-31">1109</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P2551</idno><idno type="APIS">3786</idno><note type="comment">See [BAQ9MH4T]: 193 for the main events of his life.&#13;
&#13;
Three manuscript editions of his writings and translations were deposited respectively in rNgog Blo ldan shes rab's tomb, in the monastery of gSang phu (probably in the main assembly hall), and in Drin las kyi sde (ID:4357) ([7FZ3B926]: 107).</note></person><person xml:id="person3809"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">khyung rin chen grags</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">ཁྱུང་རིན་ཆེན་གྲགས</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">jo btsun</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">jo btsun khyung</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">slang pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">glang pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">glang pha ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">slang pha ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rin chen grags</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">khyung</persName><birth notBefore="1000-01-01" notAfter="1083-12-31">after 1000 before 1083</birth><death notAfter="1170-12-31">before 1170</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P5746</idno><idno type="APIS">3809</idno><note type="comment">His dates can be approximated as 1070-1130.&#13;
&#13;
He is possibly the same person as Byang 'chad khyung (ID:6382), as [NA9AMDH6] states that Khyung rin chen grags held Byang 'chad (ID:4416) and taught Sa chen (ID:4205) there ([647FFPZR]: 543). [CJ75QQKN]: 339 (fol. 8a/170a1-3).&#13;
dMar ston's Zhib mo rdo rje ([RA3FK3BK]: 134 and 137), on the other hand, distinguish the two figures and places Sa chen's studies with Khyung at Nyang stod byang 'chad, and those with Khyung rin chen grags at Nyang stod.&#13;
&#13;
On the reference to Khyung as "slang pa/slang pha/slang pha ba" (understood as a regionalism for "glang pa/glang pha ba," see [WL39GVT5]: 940.</note></person><person xml:id="person3831"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">phywa pa chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">ཕྱྭ་པ་ཆོས་ཀྱི་སེང་གེ</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">phya pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">phya</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">cha pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">chos kyi seng+ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">phya pa chos seng ge ba</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dge' slong chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dge bshes cha pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dge bshes chen po phya pa chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">slob dpon phya pa chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">slob dpon cha pa chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">slob dpon cha pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dpal phya pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rigs pa'i dbang phyug phya pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rigs pa'i dbang phyug chos kyi seng ge</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">mkhas pa chen po cha pa</persName><birth notBefore="1109-01-01" notAfter="1109-12-31">1109</birth><death notBefore="1169-04-23" notAfter="1169-04-23">1169-04-23</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P1404</idno><idno type="APIS">3831</idno><note type="comment">The majority of the information available on Phya pa has been provided in [EZ5JXHJY] and [8S7FDMCJ]. Summaries of the same are also found in [LQZU66YX]: 37-40, [F4UK2S8P]: 2-3, and [BW9UR427]: 35-36 (in French).&#13;
&#13;
&#13;
On his birth in 1109, the year of rNgog Blo ldan shes rab (ID:3786)'s passing, see [TM8DW8WA]: 32-33, [VKBZTBWN]: 4b4-5, and [8GL7ZT7Y]: 15b2-3.&#13;
On the date of his passing in a "sa mo glang" year, see [R894SIKE]: 400, [VKBZTBWN]: 4b4-5 and 6a5. &#13;
[95UZA9Y5]: 40/3/5 (reported in [7FZ3B926]: 111 and [MPRU5QUC]: 206) specifies the day "sa mo glang gi lo'i nag pa zla ba nag po'i phyogs kyi tshes brgyad," i.e., on the 23rd of the third month.&#13;
&#13;
A list of his students is provided in [EZ5JXHJY]: 356, based on [MQB8JGAX]: 297/4, [KL9XTQWI]: I, 454, [4YEPFBIB]: 340-341, [8GL7ZT7Y], fol. 15b3, [VKBZTBWN], fol. 4b7-5a4.&#13;
[FDEWZ2NZ]: 290 also mentions the list of the four "jo sras" from bSod nams dpal's Bla ma rngog pa yab sras rim par byon pa’i rnam thar rin po che’i rgyan gyi phreng ba.&#13;
&#13;
'Gos lo tsa ba lists numerous works composed before Phya pa took the see of gSang phu; these include commentaries and summaries on the Five works of Maitreya, the Pramāṇaviniścaya, the Satyadvayavibhaṅga, Madhyamakālaṃkāra, Madhyamakāloka, and  Bodhicaryāvatāra. He mentions having seen these, the Yid kyi mun sel (ID:4503) and summaries on Madhyamaka, as well as (ID:4505) and (ID:5971). See exX0032026-04-2304-02-58 .&#13;
'Gos lo tsa ba does not mention any composition dealing with monastic discipline. &#13;
&#13;
Śākya mchog ldan ([TM8DW8WA], 12-13 and 32) mentions a (doubtful) commentary on the Pramāṇavārttika (ID:3772) in addition to the commentary on the Pramāṇaviniścaya, and three summaries (small, intermediate, large), the intermediate one being the Yid kyi mun sel (ID:4503). (Discussed in [EZ5JXHJY]: 357, translated in [8S7FDMCJ]: 59).&#13;
&#13;
Four groups of students are mentioned in Tibetan sources (which do not imply that the members of the respective groups were studying with Phya pa at the same time), with occasional variations:&#13;
&#13;
1) The "Eight Great Lions" (seng chen brgyad). Eight scholars whose names contains the expression "seng ge," likely connoting their expertise in epistemology. The standard list includes gTsang nag pa (ID:3839), Dan 'bag (ID:3840), 'Bru sha (ID:3841), rMa bya (ID:3842), rTsags/rTsegs (ID:4289), Myang/Nyang bran (ID:3844), and gNyal pa/dMyal ma (ID:6679). The eighth one is instead sometimes said to be gTsang pa 'Jam dpal (ID:6336), 'U yug pa (ID:3846), or mTshur ston (DI:4315).&#13;
&#13;
2) The "four sons of noble families" (jo sras mi bzhi) or the "three sons of noble families" (jo sras gsum). They are young noble men of the rNgog (ID:3847), 'Khon (ID:3848), Khu (ID:3849), and gNyos/sMyos (ID:3850). The four are listed in [VKBZTBWN]: 4a7 and [8GL7ZT7Y]: 15b3, whereas on 16a1 the author speaks of "jo sras gsum." The four are also listed by bSod nams dpal, see [FDEWZ2NZ]: 290.&#13;
&#13;
3) The "three wise ones" (shes rab can mi gsum) or "four wise ones" (shes rab can mi bzhi). The threefold-list in [VKBZTBWN]: 5a1 has rKong po (ID:3852), lHo pa sgom gzan (ID:3853), Par bu ba/sPar phu ba (ID:3854). 'Gar dbang grub (ID:3851) is added in the fourfold list, e.g. in [R894SIKE]: 406.&#13;
&#13;
4) The "three accomplished ones" (grub thob mi gsum). These are students who later became famous masters: Dus gsum mkhyen pa (ID:3855), Phag mo gru pa (ID:3856), gSal sto (ID:3857). The third is sometimes replaced by Zhang tshal (ID:6409), or is suggested to be the latter's direct student.</note></person><person xml:id="person4207"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">me dig pa</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">མེ་དིག་པ</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">sham po me dig pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">me tik pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rtsang me dig pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dpal me dig pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">dpal mi dig pa</persName><persName type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">me ston</persName><birth notBefore="1050-01-01" notAfter="1099-12-31">after 1050 before 1099</birth><death notBefore="1102-01-01" notAfter="1180-12-31">after 1102 before 1180</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="APIS">4207</idno></person><person xml:id="person5952"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rin chen rgyal mtshan dpal</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">རིན་ཆེན་རྒྱལ་མཚན་དཔལ</persName><birth notBefore="1040-01-01">after 1040</birth><death notBefore="1080-01-01">after 1080</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="APIS">5952</idno></person><person xml:id="person5987"><persName type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">smon lam 'od zer</persName><persName xml:lang="bo">སྨོན་ལམ་འོད་ཟེར</persName><birth notBefore="1200-01-01">after 1200</birth><death notAfter="1300-12-31">before 1300</death><sex value="M"/><nationality>Tibetan</nationality><idno type="BDRC">https://purl.bdrc.io/resource/P00KG03808</idno><idno type="APIS">5987</idno></person></listPerson><listBibl><bibl xml:id="work3774"><title type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">pramāṇaviniścaya</title><title type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">tshad ma rnam par nges pa</title><title type="alt" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">tshad ma rnam nges</title><date type="composition" notBefore="0560-01-01" notAfter="0660-12-31">after 560 before 660</date><author ref="person:3773">dharmakīrti</author><note type="isExtant">True</note><idno type="APIS">3774</idno></bibl><bibl xml:id="work5988"><title type="main" xml:lang="bo-Latn-x-wylie">rnam par nges pa'i ti ka bshad pa sbyar ba rigs pa gsal ldan</title><title xml:lang="bo">རྣམ་པར་ངེས་པའི་ཏི་ཀ་བཤད་པ་སྦྱར་བ་རིགས་པ་གསལ་ལྡན</title><textLang mainLang="bo"/><date type="composition" notBefore="1210-01-01" notAfter="1300-12-31">after 1210 before 1300</date><author ref="person:5987">smon lam 'od zer</author><note type="isExtant">True</note><note type="comment">Some references to other Tibetan scholars, who are identified in the glosses in instance (ID:5989), e.g., lo tsha (ID:3786), jo btsun (ID:3809), phya pa (ID:3831). The name "zhang ston" appears 47 times in the glosses and seems to indicate the author as it glosses for instance "rang lugs."&#13;
A gloss to the colophon in instance (ID:5989) suggests the author could be g.Yag sde paN chen (ID:3899).</note><relatedItem type="commentaryOn" target="work:3774"/><idno type="APIS">5988</idno></bibl></listBibl><listRelation><relation xml:id="rel6978" name="quotes-without-name" active="#person5987" passive="#person3809" type="positive"><desc>Evidence: <ptr target="#exX0232026-10-0503-48-08"/><ptr target="#exX0232026-03-2015-33-21"/></desc></relation><relation xml:id="rel6979" name="quotes-without-name" active="#person5987" passive="#person4207" type="positive"/><relation xml:id="rel6980" name="quotes-without-name" active="#person5987" passive="#person3831" type="positive"/><relation xml:id="rel5711" name="quotes-with-name" active="#person5952" passive="#person5987" type="positive"/><relation xml:id="rel6981" name="quotes-without-name" active="#person5987" passive="#person3786" type="positive"/><relation xml:id="rel6977" name="quotes-with-name" active="#person5987" passive="#person3786" type="positive"><desc>Evidence: <ptr target="#exX0232026-03-2015-33-20"/></desc></relation><relation xml:id="rel2272" name="author-of" active="#person5987" passive="#work5988" type="positive"/></listRelation></standOff><text>
